<body>
    <h1>Sub-advisory in Canadian WM</h1>
    <div class=3D"Section1">
        <p>In a Canadian wealth management context, the Sub-Advisory Agreem=
ent (SAA) is the foundational document that defines the boundaries of the B=
2B relationship.&nbsp; While the Primary Adviser (the "Client" of the sub-a=
dvisor) remains the registrant of record with the regulators, the SAA shift=
s specific operational and legal risks to the sub-advisor.</p>
<p>Quick Comparison of Obligations</p>
<div class=3D"table-wrap">
 <table class=3D"confluenceTable">
  <colgroup class=3D"">
   <col class=3D"">
   <col class=3D"">
   <col class=3D"">
  </colgroup>
  <tbody class=3D"">
   <tr class=3D"">
    <th class=3D"confluenceTh">Clause Type</th>
    <th class=3D"confluenceTh">Primary Adviser Obligation</th>
    <th class=3D"confluenceTh">Sub-Advisor Obligation</th>
   </tr>
   <tr class=3D"">
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">KYC/Suitability</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Full responsibility for the end-client</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">None (only responsible for the mandate)</td>
   </tr>
   <tr class=3D"">
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Reporting</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Sends statements to the investor</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Sends performance data to the Primary Advise=
r</td>
   </tr>
   <tr class=3D"">
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Audit</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Must perform "Ongoing Due Diligence."</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Must provide "SOC 1" reports or compliance c=
ertificates</td>
   </tr>
  </tbody>
 </table>
</div>
<p><br></p>
<p>Standard clauses and obligations typically found in these agreements:</p=
>
<h4 id=3D"SubadvisoryinCanadianWM-1.ScopeofAuthority&amp;InvestmentMandate"=
>1. Scope of Authority &amp; Investment Mandate</h4>
<p>This is the "what" and "how" of the relationship.</p>
<ul>
 <li data-uuid=3D"1c8fadab-0dc0-457a-98dc-5efe92e7e02c">Discretionary Autho=
rity: Explicitly grants the sub-advisor the power to buy and sell securitie=
s without seeking prior approval for every trade (within the bounds of the =
IPS)</li>
 <li data-uuid=3D"8c4cae43-4eff-4c53-9ad2-64db42df6c9f">Investment Guidelin=
es (IPS): A detailed schedule outlining permitted asset classes, concentrat=
ion limits (e.g., "no more than 5% in one issuer"), and prohibited investme=
nts (e.g., no "sin stocks" or restricted derivatives).</li>
 <li data-uuid=3D"2d3f429f-3d4b-47a9-85e7-c11a6a131c53">Proxy Voting: Defin=
es whether the sub-advisor or the Primary Adviser is responsible for voting=
 client proxies. In Canada, this is a significant fiduciary obligation.</li=
>
</ul>
<h4 id=3D"SubadvisoryinCanadianWM-2.StandardofCare&amp;RegulatoryCompliance=
">2. Standard of Care &amp; Regulatory Compliance</h4>
<ul>
 <li data-uuid=3D"49e47611-f05f-4a99-8cba-53cb5e42a436">The "Prudent Person=
" Rule: The sub-advisor must agree to exercise the degree of care, diligenc=
e, and skill that a "reasonably prudent person" would exercise in similar c=
ircumstances.</li>
 <li data-uuid=3D"b156cb42-b59b-4c2d-b736-4da46e39d8ed">NI 31-103 Complianc=
e: The sub-advisor must warrant that they are properly registered (or exemp=
t, such as via the International Adviser Exemption) and will notify the Pri=
mary Adviser immediately of any regulatory investigations or "material chan=
ges" to their firm.</li>
 <li data-uuid=3D"854b7672-6b3d-4df2-9ae8-30fa994377df">Best Execution: A m=
andatory clause requiring the sub-advisor to seek the best possible result =
for the client when executing trades, considering price, speed, and likelih=
ood of execution.</li>
</ul>
<h4 id=3D"SubadvisoryinCanadianWM-3.Representations&amp;Warranties">3. Repr=
esentations &amp; Warranties</h4>
<p>Both parties make "promises" that form the basis of the contract:</p>
<ul>
 <li data-uuid=3D"ddedd5b5-f4e3-43d3-8c0d-377493f78df5">The Sub-Advisor war=
rants they have the necessary "bench strength" (portfolio managers) and inf=
rastructure to manage the assets.</li>
 <li data-uuid=3D"db13ea29-7956-4278-88bc-1668d19c803f">The Primary Adviser=
 warrants they have the legal authority from the end-client to delegate the=
se services and that the end-client has been properly disclosed to.</li>
</ul>
<h4 id=3D"SubadvisoryinCanadianWM-4.FeesandExpenses">4. Fees and Expenses</=
h4>
<ul>
 <li data-uuid=3D"8cb3f908-e1ca-43cc-8910-79f0a43091c4">The "Sub-Advisory F=
ee": Usually a percentage of Assets Under Management (AUM), often tiered (e=
.g., 0.50% on the first $100M, 0.40% thereafter).</li>
 <li data-uuid=3D"1d8d22fe-732c-4abc-9ab2-5a962a15cde6">Expense Allocation:=
 Clearly states who pays for "extra" costs like custodial fees, audit fees,=
 or specialized research data. In Canada, "Soft Dollar" arrangements (using=
 trading commissions to pay for research) must be strictly disclosed.</li>
</ul>
<h4 id=3D"SubadvisoryinCanadianWM-5.IndemnificationandLiability">5. Indemni=
fication and Liability</h4>
<p>This is often the most heavily negotiated section.</p>
<ul>
 <li data-uuid=3D"03c11b5a-44dd-49ad-9d8e-95f255198b42">Primary Adviser=E2=
=80=99s Protection: The sub-advisor typically indemnifies the Primary Advis=
er against losses resulting from the sub-advisor's gross negligence, willfu=
l misconduct, or breach of the investment mandate.</li>
 <li data-uuid=3D"963e4581-efae-4d29-bd68-22335b6887d4">Liability Caps: Sub=
-advisors often try to limit their total liability to a multiple of the fee=
s paid (e.g., "12 months of fees"), though Primary Advisers push back on th=
is for mandate breaches.</li>
 <li data-uuid=3D"51f1ad8d-e7cd-49e2-a553-ca83797c043a">Trade Errors: Expli=
citly defines the "Trade Error Policy"=E2=80=94if the sub-advisor accidenta=
lly buys the wrong stock, they are usually responsible for making the clien=
t account whole.</li>
</ul>
<h4 id=3D"SubadvisoryinCanadianWM-6.TerminationandTransition">6. Terminatio=
n and Transition</h4>
<ul>
 <li data-uuid=3D"67afd963-4c2b-49a0-ba08-c53ed30a3087">Because the Primary=
 Adviser has a duty to the end-client, they need a "quick exit" if things g=
o wrong.</li>
 <li data-uuid=3D"918b46a0-533c-42e2-9c5c-5b1259418c36">Termination for Cau=
se: Immediate termination for regulatory breaches, bankruptcy, or "key pers=
on" departures.</li>
 <li data-uuid=3D"37be9fea-9818-40e1-8a69-b4ae8df9d6a6">Termination without=
 Cause: Typically requires 60=E2=80=9390 days' notice.</li>
 <li data-uuid=3D"b8d14abe-1f1f-4cc3-b552-9eabd29eba73">De-conversion/Trans=
ition: A clause requiring the sub-advisor to cooperate in moving the data a=
nd assets to a new manager to ensure there is no "gap" in management for th=
e end-client.</li>
</ul>
<p><br></p>
    </div>
</body>
